    "question": {
      ":question/id": "133e3585-a6a0-56a2-8fcf-8dfdba084266",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)$ varies with both $x$ and $y,$ which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope field varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, we note the following slope field does not vary with $y$ given a fixed value of $x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172040/review-1-policy-2-e0ceec0efc26ec01/assets/17592186172040/plots/d0647c9dc4664951ecefcc99ec18a9f3991a11630bb16746417fb2ec7855777d.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line, the slopes do not vary.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172040/review-1-policy-2-e0ceec0efc26ec01/assets/17592186172040/plots/8c8b83958a1a85b79fa378f4d17be4719f19b47102c376e796cbe95d90bb97e8.png)",
